---
import FieldSheetLayout from '../../layouts/FieldSheetLayout.astro';
---

<FieldSheetLayout
  slug="universal-humic-crop-calendar"
  sheetNumber="SHEET 03"
  title="Universal Humic Crop Calendar & 5 Field Precautions"
  subtitle="Stage-by-stage physiological timing blueprint (calibrated for 70–80% ISO 19822 Potassium Humate & Potassium Fulvate)."
  audience="Crop Advisors & Farm Managers"
  description="One-page printable universal humic acid crop calendar covering Pre-Planting, Germination, Vegetative Root Flush, and Pre-Flowering/Fruit Set stages plus 5 critical field precautions."
  relatedToolHref="/crops/master-dosage-table"
  relatedToolLabel="30-Crop Master Dosage Table"
>
  <!-- Stage-by-Stage Table -->
  <section class="print-avoid-break">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-base font-bold text-slate-950 mb-2 border-b border-slate-300 pb-1">
      1. Universal 4-Stage Physiological Application Calendar
    </h2>
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-xs border border-slate-300">
      <thead>
        <tr class="bg-slate-100 text-slate-950">
          <th class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-1.5 font-bold">Growth Stage</th>
          <th class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-1.5 font-bold">Preferred Form</th>
          <th class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-1.5 font-bold">Standard Rate (Ha / Acre)</th>
          <th class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-1.5 font-bold">Agronomic Objective</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2 font-bold">
            Stage 1: Pre-Planting / Basal<br />
            <span class="font-normal text-[10.5px]">(Land prep / planting furrow)</span>
          </td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2">Granular or Powder KOH Potassium Humate</td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2 font-semibold">
            3.0–6.0 kg/ha<br />(1.2–2.4 kg/acre)
          </td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2">Co-band with MAP/DAP/compost to shield phosphorus from Ca/Al fixation, build rhizosphere CEC, and aggregate topsoil.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="bg-slate-50/70">
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2 font-bold">
            Stage 2: Germination &amp; Transplanting<br />
            <span class="font-normal text-[10.5px]">(Seed coat / root dip / 5–10 DAT)</span>
          </td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2">Soluble K-Humate or K-Fulvate</td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2 font-semibold">
            Seed: 2–3 g/kg seed<br />Dip/Drench: 1–2 g/L
          </td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2">Activates root plasma-membrane H⁺-ATPase, accelerates early white feeder-root emergence, and reduces transplant shock.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2 font-bold">
            Stage 3: Active Vegetative Flush<br />
            <span class="font-normal text-[10.5px]">(Tillering, branching, canopy build)</span>
          </td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2">Drip KOH Humate (or Fulvate in hard water)</td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2 font-semibold">
            1.0–2.0 kg/ha pulse<br />(0.4–0.8 kg/ac every 14–21 d)
          </td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2">Maximizes Nitrogen &amp; Potassium Use Efficiency (NUE) during peak biomass expansion and cuts leaching in sandy soils.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="bg-slate-50/70">
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2 font-bold">
            Stage 4: Pre-Flowering &amp; Fruit Set<br />
            <span class="font-normal text-[10.5px]">(Bloom, sizing &amp; abiotic stress windows)</span>
          </td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2">Foliar Potassium Fulvate (+ Seaweed / Amino)</td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2 font-semibold">
            Foliar: 0.5–1.2 g/L<br />Drip: 1.0–1.5 kg/ha
          </td>
          <td class="border border-slate-300 px-2.5 py-2">Supports micronutrient transport (Fe, Zn, B) and antioxidant resilience (SOD/CAT/POD) 48–72 h ahead of heatwaves or dry spells.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- Liquid Conversion Shortcut -->
  <section class="print-avoid-break rounded-xl border border-slate-300 p-3 bg-slate-50/60 text-xs">
    <strong>Quick Concentration Conversion:</strong> Rates above assume <strong>70–80% solid powder/flakes</strong>. If using a <strong>12%–15% liquid humic concentrate</strong>, multiply solid kg rates by <strong>5&times; to 6&times;</strong> (e.g., 1.0 kg solid &approx; 5–6 L liquid per ha). Always verify real humic % via ISO 19822.
  </section>

  <!-- 5 Golden Precautions -->
  <section class="print-avoid-break">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-base font-bold text-slate-950 mb-2 border-b border-slate-300 pb-1">
      2. Five Essential Field Precautions
    </h2>
    <ol class="space-y-1.5 list-decimal pl-5 text-xs m-0">
      <li><strong>Never exceed label rates thinking "more is better":</strong> Excessive foliar humic concentrations (&gt; 2–3 g/L) can cause osmotic leaf scorch or leave dark residue that shades stomata.</li>
      <li><strong>Split doses outperform single megadoses:</strong> Especially in coarse sandy soils (CEC &lt; 5 meq/100g), frequent 1 kg/ha drip pulses retain far more active humus in the top 30 cm wetting bulb than one 10 kg/ha dump.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep humic acid separate from Calcium Nitrate &amp; strong acids:</strong> Flush irrigation lines 15–20 minutes between calcium/acid fertigation and alkaline potassium humate pulses to avoid emitter clogging.</li>
      <li><strong>Spray foliar fulvate during cool hours only:</strong> Apply early morning or late afternoon when temperatures are below 28 °C and stomata are open; avoid spraying wilted midday crops.</li>
      <li><strong>Humic acid complements—never replaces—NPK or agricultural lime:</strong> Position humates as a <em>soil conditioner &amp; nutrient-efficiency multiplier</em> alongside balanced fertilization and lime/gypsum where soil tests require them.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</FieldSheetLayout>
